Except for maybe the rapier (which is at least arguable), none of 
those others are at all similar to a fencing sword ("foil").  

The broadsword, for example, is more similar to a mace than it 
is to a foil, both in terms of the physical characteristics of 
the weapon itself and also in terms of how it is used.  Note 
that I'm not saying a broadsword is the same as a mace.  I'm 
saying it's nothing whatsoever like a foil.  

If I had to say what it *is* similar to, I'd say the broadsword 
is about halfway between a club and a longsword.  

The foil is kind of like a rapier, but moreso -- lighter and 
faster and whippier.  Put another way, a rapier is maybe halfway
between a foil and a short sword.

Fencing gloves, incidentally, are a real thing.  You can buy
them on Amazon, for example.  As near as I can tell, their
main distinguishing features are 1) a long wrist skirt that
would come up either over or under the sleeves of your jacket
for about six inches, maybe more, and 2) wide, padded fingers, 
even in the women's version.  Never having done any fencing, 
I have no comment on the value or purpose of either of these
distinguishing features, but they appear to be consistent 
across all major brands.

